<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Islamic calendar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Islamic lunar calendar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Islamic calendar is a lunar calendar of twelve months, dating its years from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hijra">Hijra</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> in 622 CE. It was introduced as an official dating system under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Umar. Its adoption was part of the governance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar.jpg" alt="Islamic calendar" width="320" height="482" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Islamic_Calendar_LACMA_M.89.21.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
